<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Areopagus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Council of the Areopagus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Areopagus was the ancient council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, named after the hill north-west of the Acropolis where it met. Composed of former archons, it originally had wide powers of oversight, but the reforms of Ephialtes in 462 BCE reduced it largely to a court for homicide and religious cases. It survived into Roman times, and the apostle Paul is said to have spoken before it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/areopagus.jpg" alt="Areopagus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: O.Mustafin · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Areopagus_hill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The council took its name from the Areios Pagos, the Hill of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a>, north-west of the Acropolis. The war god Ares was supposed to have been tried there by the other gods for the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a>'s son Halirrhothius. The institution's exact origin is unclear. Before the 5th century BCE it may have been a council of elders restricted to those who had held high office as archon, or it may have begun almost wholly as a homicide court. Homicide trials seem to have been held on the hill as early as the 7th century BCE, and possibly from the mid-8th.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 594 BCE Solon restructured the council along with the rest of the Athenian state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> suggests that he confirmed its competence over treason and its guardianship of the laws. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a> enlarged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boule-ancient-greece">Boule</a> to 500 men in 508/507 BCE, and there is little evidence that he changed the Areopagus. In 462/461 BCE Ephialtes may have put through reforms that left it almost nothing but a murder tribunal, in favour of the Heliaia. Most ancient authors say so, but this may be a later retrojection, since little suggests the council had done anything to provoke an attack. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>'s Eumenides of 458 BCE sets the trial of Orestes there.</p><p>In the second half of the 4th century BCE the court grew in political influence and joined the anti-Macedonian faction. In 324 BCE it investigated charges of treason and bribery against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes">Demosthenes</a> in the Harpalus affair. In Roman times it remained a body of former archons, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>'s capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 86/87 BCE it became one of the most prestigious institutions of the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> remarked that to say the Athenian state is ruled by the council, the words 'of the Areopagus' are omitted. It had ceased to operate as a political council by at least the early 5th century CE, according to Theodoret of Cyrus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The apostle Paul's sermon at the Areopagus is told in Acts 17, leading to the conversion of Dionysius the Areopagite. It is unclear whether he spoke before the council in a judicial setting or informally on the hill, since the council probably no longer met there by then but in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">agora</a> or the Stoa Basileios. After the council closed, houses, and a church and monastery dedicated to Dionysius the Areopagite, stood on the hill under Byzantine rule. The name is also used for the higher court of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-milton">John Milton</a>'s defence of press freedom, Areopagitica, takes its title from it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Areopagus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 462/461 BCE Ephialtes stripped the aristocratic <b>Council of the Areopagus</b> of most of its powers, and large popular juries, paid from public funds, took over most judicial business.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early Greek cities and in Rome aristocrats governed through a council of elders or former magistrates, such as the <b>Areopagus</b> at Athens or the Roman Senate, and through annual offices reserved for men of their own families.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>In 462 BCE Ephialtes transferred most powers of the aristocratic <b>Council of the Areopagus</b> to the Assembly, the Council and the courts, and Pericles introduced pay for jurors, so that poorer citizens could serve.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In November 1821 an assembly of local representatives met at Salona (Amfissa) and, under the guidance of the Phanariot diplomat Theodoros Negris, adopted a legal charter and created a governing council named after the ancient <b>Athenian Areopagus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/areopagus-of-eastern-continental-greece">Areopagus of Eastern Continental Greece</a></li></ul></section>
